.TH "PKGEXEC_SEMANTICS" "7" "" "libpkgexec" "Version 2.1.1"
.SH NAME
pkgexec_semantics \- native execution authority and identity rules
.SH AUTHORITY
A \f[B]pkgsource::program\f[R] remains the program authority.
\f[B]libpkgexec\f[R] seals how that program is to be executed and what evidence a backend produced.
It does not reinterpret shell material or attach package\-build or lifecycle meaning beyond the typed execution purpose.
.SH IDENTITY
Request identity includes the exact program digest and language, purpose, interpreter, root view, logical resources, closed environment, credentials, limits, cancellation policy, and required guarantees.
.PP
Evidence identity includes the request, backend capability profile, start state, termination, stream digests and byte counts, guarantees established, cleanup, and typed failure.
Diagnostic text and host observations are excluded.
.SH RESOURCE PATHS
Logical process\-visible paths are request policy.
Concrete host paths are call\-scoped backend resources and never enter semantic identities.
.SH CANCELLATION
Cancellation policy and grace period are request identity.
A live cancellation source and its token are call\-scoped operational control and have no semantic identity.
.PP
The source is bound to one exact request.
Its state changes at most once.
A controlled backend must reject a token for any other request and must not obtain cancellation intent from ambient signals, global flags, process identifiers, or controller storage.
.SH RESOURCE LIMITS
A limit value is request identity.
Every non\-empty limit policy requires the aggregate \f[B]resource_limits\f[R] guarantee and an exact guarantee for each populated kind.
Capability profiles describe only the kinds a backend can establish.
The aggregate guarantee and at least one exact kind always occur together.
Evidence may retain only guarantees present in the request; limit evidence is additionally exact by populated kind.
.PP
Resource\-limit termination is exact evidence, not an interpretation of generic program failure.
The named limit must occur in the request and its exact guarantee must have been established before process start.
.SH FAILURE
Program success does not prove isolation success.
Successful evidence requires all requested guarantees and verified cleanup.
Cleanup failure remains failure even after a zero process exit.
A rollback or cleanup attempt is not evidence of success without verification.
.SH EXCLUSIONS
The v2 core contains no Linux executor, namespaces, Landlock, cgroups, process syscalls, source acquisition, package\-input materialization, artifact creation, filesystem application, lifecycle policy, state publication, or historical compatibility behavior.
